      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "To evaluate the feasibility and safety of a virtual 3-Fr system [5-Fr sheathless-guiding catheter (GC)] for percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "The use of miniaturized devices for PCI is gaining popularity because of increased patient comfort and decreased risk of access site complications."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "From July 2010 to December 2012, consecutive patients who underwent elective PCI (planned or ad hoc PCI) at our hospital were enrolled. PCI using the virtual 3-Fr system was attempted as our initial strategy, unless a 6-Fr or larger GC was considered to be suitable [lesions with heavy calcification, large (>2 mm) side branches, or chronic total occlusion]."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Five hundred sixty-six patients underwent elective PCI during the study period, and 132 patients who met the criteria underwent PCI using the virtual 3-Fr system. Procedures using the virtual 3-Fr system were successful in 126 patients (95%); 111 (84%) were performed using the transradial approach, 110 (83%) were ad hoc procedures, and 45 (31%) were complex coronary lesions (type B2 or C). Six patients required conversion to a conventional 5- or 6-Fr sheath and catheter system. No intraoperative complications occurred, and radial artery patency was achieved in all patients who underwent transradial procedures."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "PCI using the virtual 3-Fr system is a feasible and viable alternative to conventional procedures that use a sheath and GC in appropriately selected patients. This small-caliber system may minimize endovascular trauma, particularly during transradial coronary procedures."
          }
        ]
      },
